Judge: Kennedy Center must submit plans for changes 30 days out - UPI.com

Sept. 17 (UPI) -- A judge ordered Thursday that the Trump administration must submit any plans to change the John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts in writing 30 days in advance of the work.
The decision came in response to Rep. Joyce Beatty, D-Ohio, an ex-officio Kennedy Center board member, who had requested a hearing after President Donald Trump and the board shut down the center Tuesday.
Kennedy Center Executive Director Matt Floca responded that the closure was only for seven days "unless extended" to address "acute risks to public safety" and was "separate from any Board decision related to closure for ongoing renovations."
This case is part of an ongoing lawsuit filed by Beatty to keep Trump's name off of the building.
The president took over the Kennedy Center soon after he took office in 2025, filling the board with loyalists. They announced the center was closing Tuesday after a judge ruled for a second time that he couldn't put his name on the building.
Judge Christopher Cooper also ruled against a plan to add an inscription below the sign that says "renovated and restored by Donald J. Trump."
Cooper wrote in his Thursday decision that Beatty's request for a hearing was denied.
But he added another order.
"To avoid any confusion on that score given more recent events, the Court hereby ORDERS Defendants [the Kennedy Center board] to provide such notice in writing to Plaintiffs in both cases more than 30 days before the implementation of any changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court in D.C. Preservation League [a separate case], including but not limited to any 'demolition' of the Center's main building," the ruling said.
Trump has threatened to demolish the building if he doesn't get to put his name on it, saying that it's in poor condition.
"The closing will take place immediately, however, the Renovation and Reconstruction, which is a very large and complex job, cannot begin until such time as the D.C. Circuit rules on the Board's approved name," he posted Tuesday on Truth Social. "If the ruling is a negative one, which it should not be, and is not overturned by the U.S. Supreme Court, the Reconstruction and the Renovation of the Kennedy Center will not take place."
On Wednesday night, a photo was taken through the window of Air Force One of Trump holding a poster that appeared to say, "Kennedy Center DEMOLISH." The image has circulated around social media causing alarm.
"Closing the Kennedy Center without Judge Cooper's approval appears to directly violate his order. This is an assault on the rule of law," said Norm Eisen, one of Beatty's lawyers.
"The board cannot simply ignore Judge Cooper's rulings whenever it suits them. In this country, no one -- including the president -- is above the law," he added.Rep. Beatty and Counsel Applaud Judge Cooper’s Ruling Blocking Kennedy Center Demolition | Washington Litigation Group

WASHINGTON, D.C., September 17, 2026 — Today, Judge Christopher R. Cooper issued a ruling in the ongoing Kennedy Center case that effectively prevents Donald Trump from demolishing the Kennedy Center without notice. Under Judge Cooper’s order, should the Board undertake “any changes to the scope of the project plans […] including but not limited to any ‘demolition’ of the Center’s main building,” they must provide 30 days’ advance warning. Judge Cooper additionally ordered discovery into the Board’s actions related to a potential demolition and required the Board to update the Court by September 23 regarding the latest efforts to shutter the Center.
Following this ruling in Congresswoman Joyce Beatty’s (D-OH-03) favor, she and her counsel issued the following statements:
“It is simply outrageous that we have gotten to the point of consistent judicial intervention to protect this historic institution from getting bulldozed by the President,” said Rep. Beatty. “I will continue to fight for the rule of law and defend this memorial on behalf of the American people.”
“We are grateful Judge Cooper is taking these escalating threats to the Kennedy Center seriously,” said Rep. Beatty’s counsel, Amb. Norm Eisen (ret.) of Democracy Defenders Action and Nathaniel Zelinsky of Washington Litigation Group. “Further defiance by the Trump administration would precipitate this into a full-blown constitutional crisis of the president’s own making. We are already on the precipice. Fortunately, the court and the nation are watching.”
Yesterday, Rep. Beatty filed an emergency motion before a federal district court to enforce compliance with the court’s order following the Kennedy Center’s unlawful closure. The building is currently inaccessible to the public during normal operating hours today, despite a federal court order barring Donald Trump and his handpicked board from shutting down the Kennedy Center. Under Judge Cooper’s order, Defendants must provide information regarding the closure by September 23.
Rep. Beatty, an ex officio trustee of the Kennedy Center, is represented by Nathaniel Zelinsky, Kyle Freeny, and Alex Kristofcak of Washington Litigation Group, and Norm Eisen, Steve Jonas, David W. Ogden, and Joshua Kolb of Democracy Defenders Action.
To request an interview or for questions, please contact Teresa Mozur with Democracy Defenders Action at [email protected] and Xochitl Hinojosa and Josh Schwerin for Washington Litigation Group at [email protected].
# # #
Democracy Defenders Action brings together a nonpartisan team to work with national, state, and local allies across the country to defend in real time the foundations of our democracy.
Washington Litigation Group is a nonpartisan team of attorneys dedicated to defending individuals and institutions that have been unlawfully targeted for exercising their legal rights and to fighting against governmental overreach.
