125-      <section id="what-happened-next">
126-        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The next dated steps</p>
127-        <h2 data-layer="fact">The closure and renovation claims moved onto separate tracks</h2>
128:        <p data-layer="narrative">The existing scheduling order had already opened expedited discovery on Beatty's closure claim. It set September 23 deadlines for written discovery requests and deposition notices, September 30 for written responses, October 7 for completed depositions and October 9 for the close of discovery.<Cite s="scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16" passage="Requests for Written and Document Discovery" /><Cite s="scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16" passage="All Discovery Closed" /></p>
129:        <p data-layer="narrative">The September 17 order added the update and advance-notice requirements. The September 16 scheduling order separately set deadlines for a motion to vacate the preliminary injunction and for discovery on the closure claim.<Cite s="beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17" passage="required the Board to update the Court by September 23" /><Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="more than 30 days before the implementation" /><Cite s="scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16" passage="Motion to Vacate the Preliminary" /></p>
130-        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 18, the defendants filed a motion to dissolve the May preliminary injunction and a renewed motion for partial summary judgment on Beatty's remaining closure and construction claims. Those motions asked the court for relief; they did not themselves dissolve the injunction or decide the claims.<Cite s="defendants-motion-vacate-88-2026-09-18" passage="DEFENDANTS’ MOTION TO DISSOLVE PRELIMINARY INJUNCTION" /><Cite s="defendants-summary-judgment-89-2026-09-18" passage="DEFENDANTS’ RENEWED MOTION FOR" /></p>
131-        <p data-layer="narrative">Floca says management distributed a packet on {boardReview.managementPacketDateLabel} for discussion at the {boardReview.reconsiderationDateLabel} board meeting and attached it to his declaration as Exhibit B. The declaration dates the packet to the closure deliberations but does not reproduce its contents or identify the recognition proposal.<Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="On September 13, 2026, the Center distributed to trustees a packet of materials" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="attached to this declaration as Exhibit B" /></p>
132-        <p data-layer="narrative">The defense filings agree that the board voted again to close the main building, but they quote the timing differently. The statement of facts says it would close to patrons immediately; the supporting memorandum says closure would follow dissolution of the preliminary injunction.<Cite s="defendants-statement-facts-89-2-2026-09-18" passage="shall be closed to patrons forthwith" /><Cite s="defendants-consolidated-memorandum-88-1-2026-09-18" passage="forthwith upon the dissolution of the preliminary injunction" /></p>
133-        <p data-layer="narrative">The defendants argued that phased closure would create a {funding.phasedClosureClaimLabel} funding shortfall, compared with less than {funding.fullClosureShortfallClaimLabel} for full closure. These were the defendants' assertions. Floca's declaration identifies an earlier JLL plan and Delta presentation as filed under seal. It says the finalized Delta report and certain appendices were attached as Exhibit A, while appendices with sensitive architectural drawings were excluded.<Cite s="defendants-consolidated-memorandum-88-1-2026-09-18" passage="a $300 million funding shortfall" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="appears under seal on the docket" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="correct copy of that report and certain of its appendices is attached to this declaration as" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="appendices that contain sensitive architectural drawings" /></p>
134-        <p data-layer="narrative">Later on September 18, thousands joined the Hands Around the Kennedy Center protest, and some formed a human chain around the building. The organizer invited artists, patrons, staff members, union members and volunteers.<Cite s="ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18" passage="Thousands of people joined outside" /><Cite s="ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18" passage="formed a human chain around the center" /><Cite s="action-network-hands-around-kennedy-center-2026-09-18" passage="Worked at the Center as a staff member, union member, or volunteer" /></p>
--
