95-      <section id="who-feels-it">
96-        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">What changes in practice</p>
97:        <h2 data-layer="fact">The plaintiffs get warning; the parties dispute the immediate safety closure</h2>
98-        <p data-layer="narrative">The reproduced order gives the plaintiffs in both cases a warning period before the defendants implement a covered project-scope change. It adds written notice to the earlier pledge to report a reversal of the no-demolition position.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="provide such notice in writing to Plaintiffs in both cases" /><Cite s="preservation-joint-report-62-2026-08-28" passage="change in the Board’s plans that would constitute a reversal" /></p>
99:        <p data-layer="narrative">The May opinion records Floca's estimate that between {affectedPeople.closureEstimateLow} and {affectedPeople.closureEstimateHigh} of roughly {affectedPeople.employeeEstimateTotal} employees would be affected by the closure. It also says several performance contracts would be affected, the Center had laid off at least {affectedPeople.layoffTranches} groups of staff including programming personnel, and {affectedPeople.remainingProgrammingStaff} programming staff remained.<Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="75 to 175 of roughly 300 employees at the Center would be affected" /><Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="The closure will affect several existing performance contracts" /><Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="laid off at least two tranches of staff members" /><Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="just ten members of the programming staff remain" /></p>
100-        <p data-layer="narrative">The opinion also identifies lost ticket and tourist revenue, trouble retaining audiences, the possible permanent loss of specialized arts professionals and long-term donor attrition among the downsides described in sworn testimony.<Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="lost ticket and tourist revenue; chronic challenges in audience retention" /><Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="potentially permanent loss of highly-specialized arts professionals; and long-term attrition of donors" /></p>
101-        <p data-layer="narrative">The August board plan keeps the REACH, the Center's expansion campus, open for educational and artistic programming, supports National Symphony Orchestra performances at off-site venues, and moves the Kennedy Center Honors and Mark Twain Prize off site while the main building is closed.<Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="expansion campus, known as the REACH" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="shall continue to present educational and artistic programming at the REACH" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="those performances offsite" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="Kennedy Center Honors and the Mark Twain Prize for American Humor" /></p>
102-        <p data-layer="narrative">In a public statement, the {affectedPeople.preservationPlaintiffOrganizations} preservation plaintiffs said the court should consider a receiver or replacement leadership if the Center's leaders would not protect the building. They also named a limited receiver, independent monitor or other neutral officer. They framed those measures as steps for the court to consider.<Cite s="preservation-plaintiffs-statement-2026-09-17" passage="the court should consider appointing a receiver or replacing that leadership" /><Cite s="preservation-plaintiffs-statement-2026-09-17" passage="a receiver, limited receiver, independent monitor, or other neutral officer" /></p>
103:        <p data-layer="narrative">The defendants called the {closureDispute.closureDateLabel} safety closure separate from the renovation decision. Beatty's reply answered with a chronology: the building remained open for {closureDispute.daysOpenAfterIncident} days after the {closureDispute.incidentDateLabel} plaster fall, the Walter P Moore analysis dated from {closureDispute.assessmentMonthsLabel}, and the defendants identified no meaningfully new information between the incident and the closure. The closure came the day after the board vote.<Cite s="defendants-response-86-2026-09-17" passage="separate from any Board decision related to closure for ongoing renovations" /><Cite s="beatty-reply-87-2026-09-17" passage="kept the building open for twelve days after September 4" /><Cite s="beatty-reply-87-2026-09-17" passage="identified no meaningfully new information" /><Cite s="beatty-reply-87-2026-09-17" passage="was completed in June and July of 2026" /><Cite s="beatty-reply-87-2026-09-17" passage="immediately followed the Board’s closure vote" /></p>
104-        <p data-layer="narrative">Floca identified two immediate safety grounds: an engineering assessment of the roof-terrace canopy and the September 4 failure of a five-by-four-foot piece of ceiling plaster after water damage. The same declaration reported severe corrosion in 46 of 278 panels and said one-third of the panels required immediate replacement.<Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="a roof terrace canopy assessment" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="failure of a five-by-four-foot piece of one-inch-thick ceiling plaster on September 4" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="severe corrosion in 46 panels" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="One-third of all panels require immediate replacement" /> Cooper denied the requested hearing. Neither UPI's reproduced order text nor counsel's release reports a resolution of the parties' factual dispute.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="Beatty's request for a hearing was denied" /><Cite s="beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17" passage="Judge Cooper additionally ordered discovery" /></p>
105-        <p data-layer="narrative">Bridget Kennedy-Bailey, whose declaration supported Beatty's motion, said she saw fencing block the building and saw a forklift hit exterior pillars several times. That observation was sworn testimony from one witness, not a finding by the court.<Cite s="kennedy-bailey-declaration-84-1-2026-09-16" passage="Bridget Kennedy-Bailey" /><Cite s="kennedy-bailey-declaration-84-1-2026-09-16" passage="forklift run into and hit the pillars" /></p>
106:        <p data-layer="narrative">The board's August minutes record {boardVote.votesCast} votes cast: {boardVote.inFavor} in favor and {boardVote.opposed} opposed. Another {boardVote.presentNotVoting} trustees were present but did not vote, and several were absent. The resolution directed closure of the main building for restoration and renovation and authorized legal steps to dissolve the injunction that prevented that closure.<Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="Motion carried by roll call, 23 in favor, 3 opposed" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="Present, not voting" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="seeking to dissolve the preliminary injunction" /></p>
107-        <p data-layer="narrative">That vote came with acknowledged gaps. Beatty said she received {boardReview.slidesReceived} slides {boardReview.daysBeforeMeeting} days before the meeting, Whitehouse listed {boardReview.requestedAnalyses} baseline requirements, and Larsen said the board lacked the full {boardReview.missingComparisonYearsLabel} analysis. The defendants later said the full Delta report went to trustees on {boardReview.deltaDistributionDateLabel}.<Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="only two PowerPoint slides, received two days prior" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="summarizing his request in six points" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="characterized these as baseline requirements" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="did not have the full four-year analysis" /><Cite s="defendants-statement-facts-89-2-2026-09-18" passage="On August 31, the Center’s management distributed the finalized Delta Consulting" /></p>
108-      </section>
--
125-      <section id="what-happened-next">
126-        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The next dated steps</p>
127:        <h2 data-layer="fact">The closure and renovation claims moved onto separate tracks</h2>
128:        <p data-layer="narrative">The existing scheduling order had already opened expedited discovery on Beatty's closure claim. It set September 23 deadlines for written discovery requests and deposition notices, September 30 for written responses, October 7 for completed depositions and October 9 for the close of discovery.<Cite s="scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16" passage="Requests for Written and Document Discovery" /><Cite s="scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16" passage="All Discovery Closed" /></p>
129:        <p data-layer="narrative">The September 17 order added the update and advance-notice requirements. The September 16 scheduling order separately set deadlines for a motion to vacate the preliminary injunction and for discovery on the closure claim.<Cite s="beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17" passage="required the Board to update the Court by September 23" /><Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="more than 30 days before the implementation" /><Cite s="scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16" passage="Motion to Vacate the Preliminary" /></p>
130:        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 18, the defendants filed a motion to dissolve the May preliminary injunction and a renewed motion for partial summary judgment on Beatty's remaining closure and construction claims. Those motions asked the court for relief; they did not themselves dissolve the injunction or decide the claims.<Cite s="defendants-motion-vacate-88-2026-09-18" passage="DEFENDANTS’ MOTION TO DISSOLVE PRELIMINARY INJUNCTION" /><Cite s="defendants-summary-judgment-89-2026-09-18" passage="DEFENDANTS’ RENEWED MOTION FOR" /></p>
131:        <p data-layer="narrative">Floca says management distributed a packet on {boardReview.managementPacketDateLabel} for discussion at the {boardReview.reconsiderationDateLabel} board meeting and attached it to his declaration as Exhibit B. The declaration dates the packet to the closure deliberations but does not reproduce its contents or identify the recognition proposal.<Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="On September 13, 2026, the Center distributed to trustees a packet of materials" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="attached to this declaration as Exhibit B" /></p>
132:        <p data-layer="narrative">The defense filings agree that the board voted again to close the main building, but they quote the timing differently. The statement of facts says it would close to patrons immediately; the supporting memorandum says closure would follow dissolution of the preliminary injunction.<Cite s="defendants-statement-facts-89-2-2026-09-18" passage="shall be closed to patrons forthwith" /><Cite s="defendants-consolidated-memorandum-88-1-2026-09-18" passage="forthwith upon the dissolution of the preliminary injunction" /></p>
133:        <p data-layer="narrative">The defendants argued that phased closure would create a {funding.phasedClosureClaimLabel} funding shortfall, compared with less than {funding.fullClosureShortfallClaimLabel} for full closure. These were the defendants' assertions. Floca's declaration identifies an earlier JLL plan and Delta presentation as filed under seal. It says the finalized Delta report and certain appendices were attached as Exhibit A, while appendices with sensitive architectural drawings were excluded.<Cite s="defendants-consolidated-memorandum-88-1-2026-09-18" passage="a $300 million funding shortfall" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="appears under seal on the docket" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="correct copy of that report and certain of its appendices is attached to this declaration as" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="appendices that contain sensitive architectural drawings" /></p>
134-        <p data-layer="narrative">Later on September 18, thousands joined the Hands Around the Kennedy Center protest, and some formed a human chain around the building. The organizer invited artists, patrons, staff members, union members and volunteers.<Cite s="ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18" passage="Thousands of people joined outside" /><Cite s="ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18" passage="formed a human chain around the center" /><Cite s="action-network-hands-around-kennedy-center-2026-09-18" passage="Worked at the Center as a staff member, union member, or volunteer" /></p>
135-      </section>
--
139-        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
140-        <ul data-layer="fact">
141:          <li data-absence>Whether the September 23 status report was filed, and whether the initial safety closure was extended. Searched: the admitted docket material and coverage through September 19; the report was not yet due.</li>
142-          <li data-absence>Whether the defendants have developed a project-scope change that would trigger the notice order. The accessible public filings through September 19 do not identify an approved demolition plan. Floca's declaration identifies an earlier JLL report and Delta presentation filed under seal. It says the finalized Delta report and certain appendices were filed as Exhibit A, but that exhibit did not answer through the public docket route.</li>
143:          <li data-absence>Which closure condition appears in the September 15 preliminary minutes. The two defense filings quote the resolution differently, and the preliminary minutes were not available through the public docket route.</li>
144-          <li>Trump's intent remained unresolved on September 18. The Associated Press reported that he did not directly respond when asked whether he planned to demolish the theater.<Cite s="ap-trump-kennedy-center-2026-09-18" passage="did not directly respond when a reporter asked whether he planned to demolish the theater" /></li>
145-          <li data-absence>The court's minute order as a standalone, court-hosted document. Searched: the District Court docket, CourtListener and Justia; the docket routes were unavailable to the capture registry and the Justia mirror stopped before September 17. The page therefore labels the carriers of the quoted sentence.</li>

